From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: marty <marty@fyai.co.nz>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 21:01:17 +1200
Subject: [PATCH] RetroPlayer: do not report success with a null hardware
 framebuffer

GetStreamBuffer() returned true even when GetCurrentFramebuffer() gave back 0.
The wrapper's CVideoStream::GetHwFramebuffer() allocates its cache before
calling us and keeps it whatever the answer, so one zero is remembered for the
life of the stream: every later call short-circuits on the cached value and the
client renders to framebuffer 0, which on the pool's surfaceless context is
nowhere. Black screen, no error anywhere, Kodi compositing at 60fps.

Returning false leaves the wrapper's cache unset so the client asks again on the
next frame, by which time the pool is serving buffers.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/streams/RetroPlayerRendering.cpp | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/streams/RetroPlayerRendering.cpp b/xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/streams/RetroPlayerRendering.cpp
index dc48bab..ee37272 100644
--- a/xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/streams/RetroPlayerRendering.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/streams/RetroPlayerRendering.cpp
@@ -88,7 +88,13 @@ bool CRetroPlayerRendering::GetStreamBuffer(unsigned int width,
 
   hwBuffer.framebuffer = m_renderManager.GetCurrentFramebuffer(width, height);
 
-  return true;
+  // Reporting success with a framebuffer of 0 is worse than failing. The
+  // wrapper's CVideoStream::GetHwFramebuffer() allocates its cache *before*
+  // asking us and keeps it regardless, so a single zero answer is remembered
+  // for the life of the stream and the client draws to framebuffer 0 forever -
+  // which on the pool's surfaceless context is nowhere. Failing instead leaves
+  // the cache empty and the client asks again on the next frame.
+  return hwBuffer.framebuffer != 0;
 }
 
 void CRetroPlayerRendering::AddStreamData(const StreamPacket& packet)
